Approximate Coalgebra Homomorphisms and the Emergence Surface: A Framework for Dynamical Autonomy

Ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Research Manuscript

---

Abstract

We develop a framework for emergence in dynamical systems based on approximate coalgebra homomorphisms. The central object is the defect field

\delta_{\mathcal M,\beta}(x)=d_{FY}\!\left(F\mathcal M(\alpha(x)),\,\beta(\mathcal M(x))\right),

measuring the failure of a coarse-graining \mathcal M:X\to Y to commute with coalgebra structures \alpha:X\to FX and \beta:Y\to FY. We define three closure norms and the emergence surface

C^*(\epsilon,T)=\inf\left\{C(Y):\exists(\mathcal M,\beta),\;\sup_{0\le t\le T}\Delta_t(\mathcal M,\beta)\le\epsilon\right\},

the minimum complexity for \epsilon-autonomy over horizon T. We prove: (i) monotonicity of C^*; (ii) a two-sided bound on C^* for reversible finite Markov chains via the Eckart–Young theorem, with the lower bound being the rigorous half; (iii) a Dobrushin contraction estimate for lumped chains yielding an explicit autonomy horizon; (iv) a phase transition threshold for delayed consensus; (v) a hidden-Markov counterexample separating predictive from dynamic closure. We position the framework relative to behavioural pseudometrics and quantitative coarse-graining, and state the remaining results as conjectures.

Keywords: emergence, coalgebra, coarse-graining, approximate homomorphism, Eckart–Young, Dobrushin coefficient, delayed consensus.

---

1. Introduction

The question when does a coarse-grained description acquire stable, approximately autonomous dynamics? admits a coalgebraic formulation. A dynamical system is a coalgebra (X,\alpha:X\to FX), where F encodes the behaviour type. A coarse-graining is a morphism \mathcal M:X\to Y, and the macrodescription is a coalgebra (Y,\beta:Y\to FY). Exact emergence occurs when

F\mathcal M\circ\alpha=\beta\circ\mathcal M.

Approximate emergence occurs when the two sides are close in a metric on FY.

The defect field

\delta_{\mathcal M,\beta}(x)=d_{FY}\!\left(F\mathcal M(\alpha(x)),\,\beta(\mathcal M(x))\right)

is a local, metric-space analogue of the approximation scheme used in Adámek's construction of terminal coalgebras via Cauchy sequences of approximate homomorphisms. The two operate in different categories: Adámek's construction concerns existence of a limit in the terminal coalgebra, while our defect field is a local error measure on a fixed coalgebra.

The defect field is closely related to behavioural pseudometrics in the sense of Baldan, Bonchi, Kerstan, and König, who lift functors from \mathbf{Set} to the category \mathbf{PMet} of pseudometric spaces and define behavioural distances as least fixed points of a fixed-point operator. Our one-step defect is the local version of their construction. The novelty of our framework is the temporal horizon T and the complexity tradeoff C^*(\epsilon,T).

Quantitative error bounds for coarse-graining of Markov chains have been obtained by Hilder and Sharma. Baez and Courser construct a symmetric monoidal double category of coarse-grainings of open Markov processes. The Mori–Zwanzig equation provides a rigorous decomposition of the closure defect; Zhu proves that under hypocoercivity with a finite-rank orthogonal projection P, the orthogonal semigroup is geometrically ergodic. Our Theorem 4.5 uses this result but requires an additional scale-separation parameter.

Causal emergence has been studied by Hoel, Albantakis, and Tononi and by Rosas et al. using partial information decomposition (PID). We address the non-commutativity of state and time coarse-graining observed in this literature.

This manuscript is a research paper. We prove the main results in full and state the remaining results as conjectures.

