2. Preliminaries

2.1 Coalgebras

Let \mathcal C be a category equipped with a notion of distance on objects. A behavior functor F:\mathcal C\to\mathcal C encodes the transition type. A coalgebra is a pair (X,\alpha:X\to FX). A coalgebra homomorphism from (X,\alpha) to (Y,\beta) is a morphism \mathcal M:X\to Y such that

F\mathcal M\circ\alpha=\beta\circ\mathcal M.

2.2 Behavioural pseudometrics

Following Baldan et al., we lift a functor F:\mathbf{Set}\to\mathbf{Set} to \mathbf{PMet}. For a functor F that preserves weak pullbacks, the Kantorovich lifting gives a functor F:\mathbf{PMet}\to\mathbf{PMet}. The behavioural pseudometric is the least fixed point of the operator

d'(\Phi,\Psi)=\inf\left\{\int d(x,y)\,d\mu(x,y):\mu\in\Gamma(\Phi,\Psi)\right\},

where \Gamma(\Phi,\Psi) is the set of couplings. For the probability functor \mathsf P, this is the Wasserstein distance W_1. We assume throughout that F preserves weak pullbacks and that d_{FY} is the Kantorovich lifting of a metric on Y.

2.3 Iterated coalgebras

For a coalgebra \alpha:X\to FX, the t-step map is \alpha^t:X\to F^tX. The t-step defect is

\delta_t(x)=d_{F^tY}\!\left(F^t\mathcal M(\alpha^t(x)),\,\beta^t(\mathcal M(x))\right),

where \beta^t:Y\to F^tY is the t-step macro-dynamics.

