3. The Defect Field and Closure Norms

Definition 3.1 (Defect field). Given a coalgebra (X,\alpha), a coarse-graining \mathcal M:X\to Y, and a macro-coalgebra (Y,\beta), the defect field is

\delta_{\mathcal M,\beta}(x)=d_{FY}\!\left(F\mathcal M(\alpha(x)),\,\beta(\mathcal M(x))\right).

Remark 3.2 (Order of coarse-graining and evolution). The defect compares F\mathcal M(\alpha(x)) (evolve first, then coarse-grain) with \beta(\mathcal M(x)) (coarse-grain first, then evolve). This order is correct for dynamical autonomy: we require that the macrostate at time t determine the macrostate at time t+1, which requires that \mathcal M(\alpha(x)) depend only on \mathcal M(x). This addresses the non-commutativity of state and time coarse-graining observed in the causal emergence literature.

Definition 3.3 (Closure norms). The worst-case, ensemble, and essential closure norms are

\Delta_\infty(\mathcal M,\beta)=\sup_{x\in X}\delta_{\mathcal M,\beta}(x),

\Delta_{p,\mu}(\mathcal M,\beta)=\left(\int_X\delta_{\mathcal M,\beta}(x)^p\,d\mu(x)\right)^{1/p},

\Delta_{\mathrm{ess}}(\mathcal M,\beta)=\operatorname*{ess\,sup}_{x\sim\mu}\delta_{\mathcal M,\beta}(x).

Definition 3.4 (Autonomy horizon). The autonomy horizon of (\mathcal M,\beta) at tolerance \epsilon is

T_\epsilon(\mathcal M,\beta)=\sup\left\{T:\sup_{0\le t\le T}\Delta_t(\mathcal M,\beta)\le\epsilon\right\}.

Definition 3.5 (Complexity). For a finite macro-state space Y, the complexity is C(Y)=|Y|. For a general metric space, C(Y) is the metric entropy: the minimum number of \epsilon-balls needed to cover Y.

Definition 3.6 (Emergence surface). The emergence surface is

C^*(\epsilon,T)=\inf\left\{C(Y):\exists(\mathcal M,\beta),\;\sup_{0\le t\le T}\Delta_t(\mathcal M,\beta)\le\epsilon\right\}.

Theorem 3.7 (Monotonicity). C^* is nonincreasing in \epsilon and nondecreasing in T.

Proof. If \epsilon_1\le\epsilon_2, the admissible set for \epsilon_2 contains that for \epsilon_1, so the infimum is no larger. If T_1\le T_2, the constraint for T_2 is stronger, so the infimum is no smaller. \square

