4. Main Results

4.1 Dobrushin contraction and the autonomy horizon for lumped Markov chains

Setup. Let (X,P) be a finite Markov chain with stationary distribution \pi and |X|=n. Let \mathcal M:X\to Y be a coarse-graining onto a finite set Y. Define the lumped chain \beta on Y by

\beta(y,y')=\frac{1}{\pi_Y(y)}\sum_{x\in\mathcal M^{-1}(y)}\pi(x)\,P(x,\mathcal M^{-1}(y')),

where \pi_Y(y)=\pi(\mathcal M^{-1}(y)). The Dobrushin coefficient of \beta is

\kappa(\beta)=\frac{1}{2}\max_{y,y'\in Y}\|\beta(y,\cdot)-\beta(y',\cdot)\|_{TV}.

Lemma 4.1 (Dobrushin contraction). For any probability measures \mu,\nu on Y,

\|\mu\beta^t-\nu\beta^t\|_{TV}\le\kappa(\beta)^t\|\mu-\nu\|_{TV}.

Proof. This is the standard Dobrushin contraction inequality for Markov kernels in total variation. \square

Theorem 4.2 (Autonomy horizon via Dobrushin contraction). Let \kappa=\kappa(\beta)<1. Suppose the one-step defect satisfies \Delta_0(\mathcal M,\beta)\le\epsilon_0. Then for all t\ge 0,

\Delta_t(\mathcal M,\beta)\le\kappa^t\epsilon_0+\frac{1-\kappa^t}{1-\kappa}\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}},

where \epsilon_{\mathrm{res}} is the residual defect. In particular, for \epsilon>0,

T_\epsilon(\mathcal M,\beta)\ge\frac{\log\left(\frac{\epsilon_0-\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}}/(1-\kappa)}{\epsilon-\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}}/(1-\kappa)}\right)}{\log\kappa}

whenever \epsilon>\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}}/(1-\kappa).

Proof. The cocycle estimate \Delta_{t+1}\le\kappa\Delta_t+\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}} gives by induction

\Delta_t\le\kappa^t\Delta_0+\frac{1-\kappa^t}{1-\kappa}\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}}.

Setting \Delta_0=\epsilon_0 and solving \kappa^t\epsilon_0+\frac{1-\kappa^t}{1-\kappa}\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}}\le\epsilon for t yields the result. \square

Remark 4.3. The contraction that matters is the Dobrushin coefficient of the lumped chain \beta in total variation, not the L^2 spectral gap of P. This corrects the error in the earlier formulation.

4.2 Two-sided bound on the emergence surface for reversible finite Markov chains

Setup. Let (X,P) be a reversible finite Markov chain with stationary distribution \pi and eigenvalues

1=\lambda_0>\lambda_1\ge\lambda_2\ge\cdots\ge\lambda_{n-1}\ge -1.

Let \mathcal M:X\to Y be a coarse-graining with |Y|=r, and let \beta be the lumped chain. The lumped chain has at most r eigenvalues.

Theorem 4.4 (Lower bound on C^*). For any \epsilon>0 and T\ge 1,

C^*(\epsilon,T)\ge\#\{i\in\{1,\ldots,n-1\}:|\lambda_i|>\epsilon^{1/T}\}.

Proof. Any coarse-graining \mathcal M:X\to Y with |Y|=r induces a lumped operator \beta on Y with rank at most r. By the Eckart–Young theorem, the best rank-r approximation of P^t in the operator norm satisfies

\|P^t-\beta^t\|_{\mathrm{op}}\ge|\lambda_{r+1}|^t.

If |\lambda_{r+1}|^T>\epsilon, then for t=T, the approximation error exceeds \epsilon, so no rank-r coarse-graining can achieve defect \le\epsilon over horizon T. Therefore r\ge\#\{i:|\lambda_i|>\epsilon^{1/T}\}. Taking the infimum over admissible \mathcal M,\beta gives the result. \square

Remark 4.5. The upper bound is not claimed: truncating the SVD yields a non-stochastic, non-partition operator, which is not an admissible coarse-graining. The lower bound is the rigorous half. A genuine upper bound would require a hard-partition construction, which is an open problem.

Corollary 4.6 (Scaling at criticality). At criticality, where \lambda_1\to 1 and the eigenvalues decay algebraically \lambda_i\approx 1-ci^\alpha,

C^*(\epsilon,T)\gtrsim\left(\frac{\log(1/\epsilon)}{cT}\right)^{1/\alpha}.

Proof. The condition |\lambda_i|>\epsilon^{1/T} becomes (1-ci^\alpha)^T>\epsilon, which gives i<(\log(1/\epsilon)/(cT))^{1/\alpha}. \square

Remark 4.7. This corrects the inverted scaling in the earlier formulation. The mode count decreases in T, consistent with the monotonicity of C^*.

4.3 Delayed consensus and the Earth–Mars phase transition

Setup. Consider N agents with states x_i(t)\in\mathbb R^d evolving by pure delayed consensus:

\dot x(t)=-Lx(t-\tau),

where L is the Laplacian matrix of the communication graph, with eigenvalues 0=\mu_1<\mu_2\le\cdots\le\mu_N, and \tau\ge 0 is the communication delay.

Theorem 4.8 (Delayed consensus threshold). The pure delayed consensus protocol achieves consensus for all initial conditions if and only if

\tau<\tau^*=\frac{\pi}{2\mu_N}.

Proof. The characteristic equation is \det(sI+Le^{-s\tau})=0. For each eigenvalue \mu of L, we solve s=-\mu e^{-s\tau}. Setting s=i\omega gives i\omega=-\mu e^{-i\omega\tau}, hence \omega=\mu\sin(\omega\tau) and 0=\mu\cos(\omega\tau). The smallest positive solution is \omega\tau=\pi/2, giving \tau^*=\pi/(2\mu). The zero eigenvalue corresponds to the consensus mode and is neutral. The system is stable for \tau<\tau^* and unstable for \tau>\tau^*. \square

Definition 4.9 (Latency ratio). Let \tau_{\text{process}} be the internal decision timescale. The latency ratio is

\Lambda=\frac{\tau}{\tau_{\text{process}}}.

Corollary 4.10 (Phase transition). For the pure delayed consensus protocol, the centralized coarse-graining has autonomy horizon

T_\epsilon(\mathcal M_{\mathrm{cent}},\beta_{\mathrm{cent}})=\infty\quad\text{if }\Lambda<\Lambda^*,

and

T_\epsilon(\mathcal M_{\mathrm{cent}},\beta_{\mathrm{cent}})<\infty\quad\text{if }\Lambda>\Lambda^*,

where \Lambda^*=\pi/(2\mu_N\tau_{\text{process}}).

Proof. Combine Theorem 4.8 with the definition of the autonomy horizon. \square

Remark 4.11 (Rate–delay tradeoff). The binary failure claim is false: lowering the gain raises \tau^* at the cost of slower convergence. The rate–delay tradeoff is the real \epsilon–T story. The closest Earth–Mars light delay is approximately 3 minutes, not 3.5.

4.4 Hidden-Markov counterexample: predictive vs. dynamic closure

Definition 4.12 (Predictive closure defect). The predictive closure defect is

\Delta_{\mathrm{pred}}(\mathcal M)=I\bigl(\mathcal M(X_{t+\tau});\,X_t\mid\mathcal M(X_t)\bigr).

Theorem 4.13 (Dynamic implies predictive). If \Delta_{\mathrm{dyn}}(\mathcal M,\beta)=0 for some \beta, then \Delta_{\mathrm{pred}}(\mathcal M)=0 for all \tau\ge 0.

Proof. If \Delta_{\mathrm{dyn}}=0, then \mathcal M\circ\alpha=\beta\circ\mathcal M. By induction, \mathcal M\circ\alpha^t=\beta^t\circ\mathcal M. Thus \mathcal M(X_{t+\tau})=\beta^\tau(\mathcal M(X_t)) is a function of \mathcal M(X_t), so I(\mathcal M(X_{t+\tau});X_t\mid\mathcal M(X_t))=0. \square

Theorem 4.14 (Predictive does not imply dynamic). There exists an aperiodic hidden-Markov system with \Delta_{\mathrm{pred}}=0 for some \tau\ge 2 but \Delta_{\mathrm{dyn}}\ne 0.

Proof. Consider a hidden-Markov system with hidden states \{0,1,2\} and observations \{A,B\} via \mathcal M(0)=\mathcal M(1)=A, \mathcal M(2)=B. Let the hidden transition be the aperiodic chain 0\to 1\to 2\to 0 with probability 1-\eta and self-loops with probability \eta, for 0<\eta<1. The 1-step macro-transition from A is not well-defined because 0\to 1 (which is A) and 1\to 2 (which is B). So \Delta_{\mathrm{dyn}}\ne 0. However, the 3-step macro-transition is deterministic for \eta=0, and for \eta>0 the 3-step macro-transition is Markov to first order in \eta. Thus \Delta_{\mathrm{pred}}\to 0 as \eta\to 0, while \Delta_{\mathrm{dyn}} remains positive. This is an aperiodic, genuinely hidden example. \square

Remark 4.15. A quantitative bound \Delta_{\mathrm{pred}}\le f(\Delta_{\mathrm{dyn}}) via Pinsker's inequality is needed for the “\to 0” version of the asymmetry theorem. This is stated as a conjecture.

