5. Information-Theoretic Aspects

5.1 Dynamic vs. predictive closure

Definition 5.1. The dynamic closure defect is \Delta_{\mathrm{dyn}}(\mathcal M,\beta)=\Delta_\infty(\mathcal M,\beta). The predictive closure defect is

\Delta_{\mathrm{pred}}(\mathcal M)=I\bigl(\mathcal M(X_{t+\tau});\,X_t\mid\mathcal M(X_t)\bigr).

Conjecture 5.2 (Asymmetry). Under a mixing assumption, \Delta_{\mathrm{dyn}}\to 0 implies \Delta_{\mathrm{pred}}\to 0. The converse fails in general.

Status. The exact version is proven (Theorem 4.13). The quantitative version requires a Pinsker-type bound, which is open.

5.2 Causal emergence via PID

Definition 5.3 (Partial information decomposition). Following Rosas et al., the PID of a collection of source variables X^1,\ldots,X^k with respect to a target Y decomposes I(Y;X^1,\ldots,X^k) into redundant, unique, and synergistic components:

I(Y;X^1,\ldots,X^k)=\mathrm{Red}+\sum_j\mathrm{Unq}_j+\mathrm{Syn}.

The synergistic component \mathrm{Syn} measures information about Y available only when the sources are considered jointly.

Definition 5.4 (Causal emergence). A macrostate \mathcal M(X) is causally emergent if the synergistic component \mathrm{Syn} of the PID decomposition of I(Y;X^1,\ldots,X^k) with respect to the microscopic components X^j is positive, and \mathcal M(X) captures this synergy.

Remark 5.5. The earlier definition using the pair (X,\mathcal M(X)) is degenerate because \mathcal M(X) is a function of X. The correct definition uses the microscopic components X^j.

Conjecture 5.6 (Causal emergence criterion). If \Delta_{\mathrm{pred}}=0 and \mathrm{Syn}>0 in the PID decomposition of the microscopic components, then the macrostate is causally emergent.

Status. Open.

