7. Discussion

7.1 The central object

The heart of the framework is the emergence surface

C^*(\epsilon,T)=\inf\left\{C(Y):\exists(\mathcal M,\beta),\;\sup_{0\le t\le T}\Delta_t(\mathcal M,\beta)\le\epsilon\right\}.

This object quantifies the minimum complexity required for \epsilon-autonomy over horizon T.

7.2 The central approximation

The heart of the mathematics is

\mathcal M\circ\alpha\approx\beta\circ\mathcal M.

Emergence occurs when the future behaviour of a complex system can be represented, with controlled error and over a specified temporal horizon, by a lower-complexity state space whose dynamics is approximately autonomous.

7.3 Relation to existing work

The defect field is a local version of the behavioural pseudometrics of Baldan et al. The temporal horizon and complexity tradeoff are new. The Dobrushin contraction theorem (Theorem 4.2) and the Eckart–Young lower bound (Theorem 4.4) are the rigorous core. The delayed consensus threshold (Theorem 4.8) is a standard result in control theory, applied here to the Earth–Mars problem.

7.4 What is proven and what is conjectural

The following results are proven in full: monotonicity of C^* (Theorem 3.7); Dobrushin contraction and autonomy horizon (Theorem 4.2); the lower bound on C^* (Theorem 4.4); the scaling at criticality (Corollary 4.6); the delayed consensus threshold (Theorem 4.8); and the hidden-Markov counterexample (Theorem 4.14). The upper bound on C^*, the general approximate closure theorem, the quantitative asymmetry theorem, and the causal emergence criterion are conjectural.

7.5 Future work

The most pressing open problems are a genuine upper bound on C^* via hard-partition construction, a quantitative Pinsker-type bound for the predictive–dynamic asymmetry, and a correct PID-based definition of causal emergence using the microscopic components X^j.

