Appendix A: Proofs for Chapter 3

A.1 Proof of Theorem 3.7 (Monotonicity of the emergence surface)

Theorem 3.7. The emergence surface C^*(\epsilon,T) is nonincreasing in \epsilon and nondecreasing in T.

Proof. Fix T>0. Define the admissible set

\mathcal A(\epsilon,T)=\left\{(\mathcal M,\beta):\sup_{0\le t\le T}\Delta_t(\mathcal M,\beta)\le\epsilon\right\}.

If \epsilon_1\le\epsilon_2, then for every (\mathcal M,\beta)\in\mathcal A(\epsilon_1,T) we have \sup_{0\le t\le T}\Delta_t(\mathcal M,\beta)\le\epsilon_1\le\epsilon_2, so (\mathcal M,\beta)\in\mathcal A(\epsilon_2,T). Hence \mathcal A(\epsilon_1,T)\subseteq\mathcal A(\epsilon_2,T).

The complexity functional C(Y) depends only on the target space Y, so the infimum over a larger admissible set is no larger:

C^*(\epsilon_2,T)=\inf_{(\mathcal M,\beta)\in\mathcal A(\epsilon_2,T)}C(Y)\le\inf_{(\mathcal M,\beta)\in\mathcal A(\epsilon_1,T)}C(Y)=C^*(\epsilon_1,T).

Thus C^* is nonincreasing in \epsilon.

Now fix \epsilon>0. If T_1\le T_2, then for every (\mathcal M,\beta)\in\mathcal A(\epsilon,T_2) we have \sup_{0\le t\le T_1}\Delta_t(\mathcal M,\beta)\le\sup_{0\le t\le T_2}\Delta_t(\mathcal M,\beta)\le\epsilon, so (\mathcal M,\beta)\in\mathcal A(\epsilon,T_1). Hence \mathcal A(\epsilon,T_2)\subseteq\mathcal A(\epsilon,T_1).

Therefore

C^*(\epsilon,T_1)=\inf_{(\mathcal M,\beta)\in\mathcal A(\epsilon,T_1)}C(Y)\le\inf_{(\mathcal M,\beta)\in\mathcal A(\epsilon,T_2)}C(Y)=C^*(\epsilon,T_2).

Thus C^* is nondecreasing in T. \square

