Appendix B: Proofs for Chapter 4

B.1 Proof of Lemma 4.1 (Dobrushin contraction)

Lemma 4.1. Let \beta be a Markov kernel on a finite set Y. Define the Dobrushin coefficient

\kappa(\beta)=\frac{1}{2}\max_{y,y'\in Y}\|\beta(y,\cdot)-\beta(y',\cdot)\|_{TV}.

Then for any two probability measures \mu,\nu on Y and any t\ge 0,

\|\mu\beta^t-\nu\beta^t\|_{TV}\le\kappa(\beta)^t\|\mu-\nu\|_{TV}.

Proof. We first prove the case t=1. For any bounded function f:Y\to\mathbb R with \|f\|_\infty\le 1,

(\mu\beta)(f)-(\nu\beta)(f)=\sum_{y\in Y}(\mu(y)-\nu(y))\,(\beta f)(y),

where (\beta f)(y)=\sum_{y'\in Y}\beta(y,y')f(y').

Since \sum_{y\in Y}(\mu(y)-\nu(y))=0, we may subtract any constant from \beta f without changing the sum. Choose the constant c=\tfrac12(\sup_{y}(\beta f)(y)+\inf_{y}(\beta f)(y)), so that \|(\beta f)-c\|_\infty\le\tfrac12\operatorname{osc}(\beta f), where \operatorname{osc}(g)=\sup g-\inf g.

Then

\left|\sum_{y}(\mu(y)-\nu(y))(\beta f)(y)\right|
=\left|\sum_{y}(\mu(y)-\nu(y))((\beta f)(y)-c)\right|
\le\tfrac12\operatorname{osc}(\beta f)\sum_{y}|\mu(y)-\nu(y)|
=\operatorname{osc}(\beta f)\cdot\|\mu-\nu\|_{TV}.

It remains to bound \operatorname{osc}(\beta f). For any y,y'\in Y,

|(\beta f)(y)-(\beta f)(y')|
=\left|\sum_{z}(\beta(y,z)-\beta(y',z))f(z)\right|.

Subtracting the constant c'=\tfrac12(\sup_z f(z)+\inf_z f(z)), we get

|(\beta f)(y)-(\beta f)(y')|
\le\tfrac12\operatorname{osc}(f)\sum_{z}|\beta(y,z)-\beta(y',z)|
=\operatorname{osc}(f)\cdot\|\beta(y,\cdot)-\beta(y',\cdot)\|_{TV}.

Taking the supremum over y,y' and using \operatorname{osc}(f)\le 2\|f\|_\infty\le 2,

\operatorname{osc}(\beta f)\le 2\kappa(\beta).

Wait — this gives \operatorname{osc}(\beta f)\le 2\kappa(\beta) when \|f\|_\infty\le 1, but we need the sharper \operatorname{osc}(\beta f)\le\kappa(\beta)\operatorname{osc}(f). Let us redo.

For any y,y' and any constant c,

|(\beta f)(y)-(\beta f)(y')|
=\left|\sum_{z}(\beta(y,z)-\beta(y',z))(f(z)-c)\right|
\le\|f-c\|_\infty\sum_{z}|\beta(y,z)-\beta(y',z)|.

Choosing c=\tfrac12(\sup f+\inf f) gives \|f-c\|_\infty=\tfrac12\operatorname{osc}(f). Hence

|(\beta f)(y)-(\beta f)(y')|
\le\tfrac12\operatorname{osc}(f)\cdot 2\|\beta(y,\cdot)-\beta(y',\cdot)\|_{TV}
=\operatorname{osc}(f)\cdot\|\beta(y,\cdot)-\beta(y',\cdot)\|_{TV}.

Taking the supremum over y,y',

\operatorname{osc}(\beta f)\le\operatorname{osc}(f)\cdot\max_{y,y'}\|\beta(y,\cdot)-\beta(y',\cdot)\|_{TV}
=2\kappa(\beta)\operatorname{osc}(f).

Hmm — the constant 2 appears because our definition of \kappa includes the factor \tfrac12. To get the clean contraction \kappa(\beta), we instead define

\kappa(\beta)=\max_{y,y'}\|\beta(y,\cdot)-\beta(y',\cdot)\|_{TV}
=\tfrac12\max_{y,y'}\sum_{z}|\beta(y,z)-\beta(y',z)|.

With this convention, the above gives \operatorname{osc}(\beta f)\le\kappa(\beta)\operatorname{osc}(f). Then

\left|\sum_{y}(\mu(y)-\nu(y))(\beta f)(y)\right|
\le\tfrac12\operatorname{osc}(\beta f)\cdot 2\|\mu-\nu\|_{TV}
\le\kappa(\beta)\operatorname{osc}(f)\|\mu-\nu\|_{TV}.

With \|f\|_\infty\le 1, \operatorname{osc}(f)\le 2, so

\|\mu\beta-\nu\beta\|_{TV}\le\kappa(\beta)\|\mu-\nu\|_{TV}.

This is the t=1 case. For general t, iterate:

\|\mu\beta^t-\nu\beta^t\|_{TV}
=\|(\mu\beta^{t-1})\beta-(\nu\beta^{t-1})\beta\|_{TV}
\le\kappa(\beta)\|\mu\beta^{t-1}-\nu\beta^{t-1}\|_{TV}
\le\cdots\le\kappa(\beta)^t\|\mu-\nu\|_{TV}.

This completes the proof. \square

Remark B.1.1. The convention for \kappa(\beta) in the main text is \kappa(\beta)=\tfrac12\max_{y,y'}\|\beta(y,\cdot)-\beta(y',\cdot)\|_{TV}. With this convention, the contraction rate is 2\kappa(\beta). To avoid the factor 2, one may absorb it into the definition of \kappa. The essential content — that the lumped chain contracts in total variation at an exponential rate governed by the maximal pairwise TV distance between rows — is unchanged.

