B.2 Proof of Theorem 4.2 (Autonomy horizon via Dobrushin contraction)

Theorem 4.2. Let \kappa=\kappa(\beta)<1. Suppose the one-step defect satisfies \Delta_0(\mathcal M,\beta)\le\epsilon_0. Then for all t\ge 0,

\Delta_t(\mathcal M,\beta)\le\kappa^t\epsilon_0+\frac{1-\kappa^t}{1-\kappa}\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}},

where \epsilon_{\mathrm{res}} is the residual defect. In particular, for \epsilon>0,

T_\epsilon(\mathcal M,\beta)\ge\frac{\log\left(\frac{\epsilon-\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}}/(1-\kappa)}{\epsilon_0-\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}}/(1-\kappa)}\right)}{\log\kappa}

whenever \epsilon>\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}}/(1-\kappa) and \epsilon_0>\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}}/(1-\kappa).

Proof. We proceed by induction on t. The cocycle estimate for the defect is

\Delta_{t+1}\le\kappa\Delta_t+\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}},\qquad t\ge 0.

For t=0 the claimed inequality is \Delta_0\le\epsilon_0, which is the hypothesis. Suppose the inequality holds for t. Then

\Delta_{t+1}\le\kappa\Delta_t+\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}}
\le\kappa\left(\kappa^t\epsilon_0+\frac{1-\kappa^t}{1-\kappa}\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}}\right)+\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}}
=\kappa^{t+1}\epsilon_0+\frac{\kappa(1-\kappa^t)}{1-\kappa}\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}}+\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}}.

Now

\frac{\kappa(1-\kappa^t)}{1-\kappa}+1
=\frac{\kappa-\kappa^{t+1}+1-\kappa}{1-\kappa}
=\frac{1-\kappa^{t+1}}{1-\kappa}.

Hence \Delta_{t+1}\le\kappa^{t+1}\epsilon_0+\frac{1-\kappa^{t+1}}{1-\kappa}\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}}, completing the induction.

For the horizon bound, we require \kappa^t\epsilon_0+\frac{1-\kappa^t}{1-\kappa}\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}}\le\epsilon. Rearranging,

\kappa^t\left(\epsilon_0-\frac{\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}}}{1-\kappa}\right)
\le\epsilon-\frac{\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}}}{1-\kappa}.

If \epsilon_0-\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}}/(1-\kappa)>0 and \epsilon-\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}}/(1-\kappa)>0, divide both sides:

\kappa^t\le\frac{\epsilon-\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}}/(1-\kappa)}{\epsilon_0-\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}}/(1-\kappa)}.

Taking logarithms (note \log\kappa<0 since \kappa<1, so the inequality reverses):

t\ge\frac{\log\left(\frac{\epsilon-\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}}/(1-\kappa)}{\epsilon_0-\epsilon_{\mathrm{res}}/(1-\kappa)}\right)}{\log\kappa}.

The right-hand side is positive whenever \epsilon<\epsilon_0. This gives the stated lower bound on T_\epsilon. \square

