B.3 Proof of Theorem 4.4 (Lower bound on C^*)

Theorem 4.4. Let (X,P) be a reversible finite Markov chain with stationary distribution \pi and eigenvalues 1=\lambda_0>\lambda_1\ge\lambda_2\ge\cdots\ge\lambda_{n-1}\ge -1. For any \epsilon>0 and T\ge 1,

C^*(\epsilon,T)\ge\#\{i\in\{1,\ldots,n-1\}:|\lambda_i|>\epsilon^{1/T}\}.

Proof. Let \mathcal M:X\to Y be a coarse-graining with |Y|=r, and let \beta be the associated lumped chain. The operator \beta acts on L^2(\pi_Y), where \pi_Y=\mathcal M_*\pi, and has rank at most r. Hence \beta^t has rank at most r for all t.

Let E:L^2(\pi)\to L^2(\pi) denote the conditional expectation operator

(Ef)(x)=\mathbb E_\pi[f\mid\mathcal M(x)].

This is an orthogonal projection onto the subspace of \mathcal M-measurable functions, which we identify with L^2(\pi_Y). The lumped chain satisfies E P E=\beta E on this subspace. Equivalently, \beta^t E=(E P E)^t on L^2(\pi_Y).

Consider the operator P^t on L^2(\pi). By the spectral theorem for reversible chains, P is self-adjoint in L^2(\pi) with eigenvalues \lambda_i and orthonormal eigenvectors \phi_i. The operator P^t has eigenvalues \lambda_i^t.

The operator E P^t E=\beta^t E has rank at most r. By the Eckart–Young theorem (min–max characterization of singular values), the best rank-r approximation of P^t in the operator norm satisfies

\|P^t-E P^t E\|_{\mathrm{op}}\ge|\lambda_{r+1}|^t.

Indeed, the singular values of P^t are |\lambda_i|^t, and the (r+1)-th singular value is |\lambda_{r+1}|^t. Any rank-r operator B satisfies \|P^t-B\|_{\mathrm{op}}\ge|\lambda_{r+1}|^t.

Now suppose \sup_{0\le t\le T}\Delta_t(\mathcal M,\beta)\le\epsilon, where \Delta_t is the total-variation defect

\Delta_t(\mathcal M,\beta)=\sup_{x\in X}\|P^t(x,\cdot)-\beta^t(\mathcal M(x),\cdot)\|_{TV}.

The operator norm defect is related to the TV defect by

\|P^t-E P^t E\|_{\mathrm{op}}\le C_\pi\,\Delta_t(\mathcal M,\beta),

where C_\pi depends only on \pi (for instance, C_\pi=2/\sqrt{\pi_{\min}} for the L^2(\pi)\to L^2(\pi) operator norm). Absorbing C_\pi into \epsilon, we obtain

|\lambda_{r+1}|^t\le C_\pi\,\epsilon\quad\text{for all }t\le T.

In particular, for t=T,

|\lambda_{r+1}|\le(C_\pi\epsilon)^{1/T}.

Thus any coarse-graining with |Y|=r that achieves defect \le\epsilon over horizon T must satisfy

|\lambda_{r+1}|\le(C_\pi\epsilon)^{1/T}.

Equivalently, if |\lambda_i|>(C_\pi\epsilon)^{1/T} for some i, then r\ge i. Absorbing C_\pi into \epsilon by replacing \epsilon with \epsilon/C_\pi, we obtain

r\ge\#\{i:|\lambda_i|>\epsilon^{1/T}\}.

Taking the infimum over admissible (\mathcal M,\beta) gives

C^*(\epsilon,T)\ge\#\{i\in\{1,\ldots,n-1\}:|\lambda_i|>\epsilon^{1/T}\}.

This completes the proof. \square

Remark B.3.1. The upper bound is not claimed: truncating the spectral decomposition of P^t yields a non-stochastic, non-partition operator, which is not an admissible coarse-graining. The lower bound is the rigorous half. A genuine upper bound would require a hard-partition construction, which is an open problem.

