B.4 Proof of Corollary 4.6 (Scaling at criticality)

Corollary 4.6. At criticality, where \lambda_1\to 1 and the eigenvalues decay algebraically \lambda_i\approx 1-ci^\alpha for some \alpha>0 and c>0,

C^*(\epsilon,T)\gtrsim\left(\frac{\log(1/\epsilon)}{cT}\right)^{1/\alpha}.

Proof. By Theorem 4.4,

C^*(\epsilon,T)\ge\#\{i:|\lambda_i|>\epsilon^{1/T}\}.

With \lambda_i\approx 1-ci^\alpha, the condition |\lambda_i|>\epsilon^{1/T} becomes

1-ci^\alpha>\epsilon^{1/T}.

Rearranging,

ci^\alpha<1-\epsilon^{1/T}.

For small \epsilon and large T, 1-\epsilon^{1/T}\approx\log(1/\epsilon)/T (using 1-e^{-x}\approx x with x=\log(1/\epsilon)/T). Hence

i^\alpha<\frac{\log(1/\epsilon)}{cT},

so

i<\left(\frac{\log(1/\epsilon)}{cT}\right)^{1/\alpha}.

Thus the number of eigenvalues satisfying the condition is at least of order \left(\log(1/\epsilon)/(cT)\right)^{1/\alpha}, giving the stated lower bound. \square

Remark B.4.1. This corrects the inverted scaling in the earlier formulation. The mode count decreases in T, consistent with the monotonicity of C^* in T (Theorem 3.7).

