B.5 Proof of Theorem 4.8 (Delayed consensus threshold)

Theorem 4.8. Consider the pure delayed consensus protocol

\dot x(t)=-Lx(t-\tau),

where L is the Laplacian matrix of the communication graph with eigenvalues 0=\mu_1<\mu_2\le\cdots\le\mu_N, and \tau\ge 0 is the communication delay. The protocol achieves consensus for all initial conditions if and only if

\tau<\tau^*=\frac{\pi}{2\mu_N}.

Proof. Taking the Laplace transform of \dot x(t)=-Lx(t-\tau) gives the characteristic equation

\det(sI+Le^{-s\tau})=0.

Since L is symmetric, it is diagonalizable by an orthogonal matrix. The characteristic equation decouples into scalar equations

s+\mu_k e^{-s\tau}=0,\qquad k=1,\ldots,N,

where \mu_k are the eigenvalues of L.

For \mu_1=0, the equation is s=0, corresponding to the consensus mode (neutrally stable).

For \mu_k>0, we analyze the roots of s+\mu e^{-s\tau}=0. The system is stable if and only if all roots have negative real part. The boundary of stability occurs when a root crosses the imaginary axis, i.e., s=i\omega for some \omega\in\mathbb R.

Substituting s=i\omega:

i\omega+\mu e^{-i\omega\tau}=0
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
i\omega=-\mu e^{-i\omega\tau}=-\mu(\cos(\omega\tau)-i\sin(\omega\tau)).

Equating real and imaginary parts:

0=-\mu\cos(\omega\tau),\qquad \omega=\mu\sin(\omega\tau).

From the first equation, \cos(\omega\tau)=0, so \omega\tau=\pi/2+k\pi for k\in\mathbb Z. From the second equation, \omega=\mu\sin(\omega\tau). Since \omega>0 and \mu>0, we need \sin(\omega\tau)>0. The smallest positive solution is \omega\tau=\pi/2, giving \omega=\mu.

Thus the critical delay for mode \mu is \tau_c(\mu)=\pi/(2\mu). The most restrictive mode is the largest eigenvalue \mu_N, giving

\tau^*=\frac{\pi}{2\mu_N}.

For \tau<\tau^*, all roots have negative real part (stable). For \tau>\tau^*, at least one root has positive real part (unstable). This completes the proof. \square

---

B.6 Proof of Corollary 4.10 (Phase transition)

Corollary 4.10. For the pure delayed consensus protocol, the centralized coarse-graining has autonomy horizon

T_\epsilon(\mathcal M_{\mathrm{cent}},\beta_{\mathrm{cent}})=\infty\quad\text{if }\Lambda<\Lambda^*,

and

T_\epsilon(\mathcal M_{\mathrm{cent}},\beta_{\mathrm{cent}})<\infty\quad\text{if }\Lambda>\Lambda^*,

where \Lambda^*=\pi/(2\mu_N\tau_{\text{process}}).

Proof. By Theorem 4.8, the system converges to consensus for \tau<\tau^* and diverges for \tau>\tau^*. In the convergent regime, the macrostate (the consensus value) is reached asymptotically, and the centralized coarse-graining is autonomous indefinitely, so T_\epsilon=\infty for any \epsilon>0. In the divergent regime, the macrostate does not stabilize, and the defect eventually exceeds any fixed \epsilon>0, so T_\epsilon<\infty. The threshold in terms of the latency ratio is \Lambda^*=\tau^*/\tau_{\text{process}}=\pi/(2\mu_N\tau_{\text{process}}). \square

Remark B.6.1. The binary failure claim is false: lowering the gain raises \tau^* at the cost of slower convergence. The rate–delay tradeoff is the real \epsilon–T story. The closest Earth–Mars light delay is approximately 3 minutes, not 3.5.

