B.7 Proof of Theorem 4.13 (Dynamic implies predictive)

Theorem 4.13. If \Delta_{\mathrm{dyn}}(\mathcal M,\beta)=0 for some \beta, then \Delta_{\mathrm{pred}}(\mathcal M)=0 for all \tau\ge 0.

Proof. If \Delta_{\mathrm{dyn}}=0, then \mathcal M\circ\alpha=\beta\circ\mathcal M. By induction,

\mathcal M\circ\alpha^t=\beta^t\circ\mathcal M\qquad\text{for all }t\ge 0.

In particular, for t=\tau,

\mathcal M(X_{t+\tau})=\mathcal M(\alpha^\tau(X_t))=\beta^\tau(\mathcal M(X_t)).

Thus \mathcal M(X_{t+\tau}) is a measurable function of \mathcal M(X_t), hence \sigma(\mathcal M(X_t))-measurable. Therefore

I\bigl(\mathcal M(X_{t+\tau});\,X_t\mid\mathcal M(X_t)\bigr)=0,

since conditioning on \mathcal M(X_t) renders \mathcal M(X_{t+\tau}) deterministic. This holds for all \tau\ge 0. \square

