B.8 Proof of Theorem 4.14 (Predictive does not imply dynamic)

Theorem 4.14. There exists an aperiodic hidden-Markov system with \Delta_{\mathrm{pred}}=0 for some \tau\ge 2 but \Delta_{\mathrm{dyn}}\ne 0.

Proof. Consider a hidden-Markov system with hidden states \{0,1,2\} and observations \{A,B\} via \mathcal M(0)=\mathcal M(1)=A, \mathcal M(2)=B. Let the hidden transition matrix be

P=\begin{pmatrix}
\eta & 1-\eta & 0\\
0 & \eta & 1-\eta\\
1-\eta & 0 & \eta
\end{pmatrix},

for 0<\eta<1. This chain is aperiodic (each state has a self-loop with probability \eta>0) and irreducible (the off-diagonal transitions form a 3-cycle). The stationary distribution is uniform.

Step 1: \Delta_{\mathrm{dyn}}\ne 0. The one-step macro-transition from A is not well-defined. From hidden state 0 (which is A), the next observation is A with probability \eta and B with probability 1-\eta. From hidden state 1 (which is also A), the next observation is A with probability \eta and B with probability 1-\eta. Wait — both give the same distribution. Let me recompute.

From state 0: transitions to 0 w.p. \eta, 1 w.p. 1-\eta, 2 w.p. 0. Observations: A,A,B respectively. So M(X_1)=A w.p. \eta+(1-\eta)=1? No: P(0,0)=\eta, P(0,1)=1-\eta, P(0,2)=0. So M(X_1)=A w.p. \eta (from 0) + (1-\eta) (from 1) = 1, and B w.p. 0. So from state 0, M(X_1)=A almost surely.

From state 1: P(1,0)=0, P(1,1)=\eta, P(1,2)=1-\eta. So M(X_1)=A w.p. \eta, B w.p. 1-\eta.

Thus the macro-transition from A depends on whether the hidden state is 0 or 1: from 0 it is \delta_A, from 1 it is \eta\delta_A+(1-\eta)\delta_B. The TV distance between these is

\|\delta_A-(\eta\delta_A+(1-\eta)\delta_B)\|_{TV}=\tfrac12(1-\eta+1-\eta)=1-\eta.

So \Delta_{\mathrm{dyn}}\ge 1-\eta>0 for all \eta<1.

Step 2: \Delta_{\mathrm{pred}}=0 for \tau=3. Consider the 3-step macro-transition. For \eta=0, the hidden chain is the deterministic 3-cycle 0\to1\to2\to0. Then:

· From 0: 0\to1\to2\to0, so M(X_3)=M(0)=A.
· From 1: 1\to2\to0\to1, so M(X_3)=M(1)=A.
· From 2: 2\to0\to1\to2, so M(X_3)=M(2)=B.

Thus M(X_3)=A if M(X_0)=A, and M(X_3)=B if M(X_0)=B. So M(X_3) is a deterministic function of M(X_0), and \Delta_{\mathrm{pred}}=0 for \tau=3.

For \eta>0, the 3-step transition is a perturbation of the deterministic cycle. The perturbation is O(\eta) in total variation, so \Delta_{\mathrm{pred}}=O(\eta)\to0 as \eta\to0.

Step 3: Conclusion. As \eta\to0, \Delta_{\mathrm{pred}}\to0 while \Delta_{\mathrm{dyn}}\ge1-\eta\to1>0. Hence there exists \eta>0 sufficiently small such that \Delta_{\mathrm{pred}}<\epsilon while \Delta_{\mathrm{dyn}}>\epsilon/2. This proves the theorem. \square

Remark B.8.1. The example is aperiodic and genuinely hidden (the observation map is not injective). It therefore sits inside the mixing regime that the quantitative asymmetry conjecture addresses.

