Appendix C: Proofs for the Information-Theoretic Results

C.1 Proof of Theorem 5.5 (Causal emergence criterion)

Theorem 5.5. If \Delta_{\mathrm{pred}}=0 and \mathrm{Syn}>0 in the PID decomposition of the microscopic components X^j with respect to the target Y, then the macrostate is causally emergent.

Proof. By Definition 5.3, the PID decomposition of I(Y;X^1,\ldots,X^k) is

I(Y;X^1,\ldots,X^k)=\mathrm{Red}+\sum_j\mathrm{Unq}_j+\mathrm{Syn}.

If \mathrm{Syn}>0, then there is information about Y that is available only when the sources X^1,\ldots,X^k are considered jointly, not from any individual source alone.

By Definition 5.4, a macrostate \mathcal M(X) is causally emergent if the synergistic component \mathrm{Syn} of the PID decomposition with respect to the microscopic components X^j is positive, and \mathcal M(X) captures this synergy.

If \Delta_{\mathrm{pred}}=0, then \mathcal M(X_{t+\tau}) is a sufficient statistic for X_t given \mathcal M(X_t), so the macrostate preserves the predictive information about the future. Combined with \mathrm{Syn}>0, the macrostate captures the synergistic information that cannot be reduced to any individual microscopic component. Hence the macrostate is causally emergent. \square

Remark C.1.1. The earlier definition using the pair (X,\mathcal M(X)) is degenerate because \mathcal M(X) is a function of X. The correct definition uses the microscopic components X^j, as in the PID framework of Rosas et al.


